t recognize the progress  that has been made and i'm happy to let  give you that opportunity but at the  same time 4 000 covered 19 positive  people  went more than a week without being  contacted by tests and traits  it's not exactly not perfect of course  but that is a failure  and at this time when we are spending so  much money on it  i think people want to hear a government  minister concede that  well i think government ministers and  indeed died of harding have said very  clearly at times when the system has  been under stress that's not  something that we want to see that's why  we're putting the extra resources in as  you said 12 billion pounds  the number of tests have gone up  considerably from the beginning they're  continuing to ramp up  and the performance metrics are  improving we now you know it's just  i think on average when i last saw no  one is going on average more than about  four miles  for a test the vast majority of tests  are coming back the next day  and the testing capacity and tests  carried out are ramping up every day  that's all good progress you're right  there's more to do and i can assure you  it remains a number one priority of  focus for us  i suppose the problem with the  government is this very similar to the  situation as with a levels people were  warning you  about the problems with the excel  spreadsheet the problems with putting  outdated hardware running  outdated software at the crunch point in  terms of getting the data  into the system joanna vienna fellow in  epigenetics at the university of  birmingham said this  when we started at the lighthouse labs  in march in march  we advised against analyzing the results  in excel and said  multiple times they should recruit  bioinformaticians to deal with the data  why was all that why were we faffing  around with excel  when the scientists were telling us not  to well i think also that the system  we've designed has been designed in  conjunction with our scientists we have  