ance to the  situation we confront  how are we going to get out of this from  from your speech yesterday it seemed  pretty clear that we're not going to be  seeing a return to  austerity to cuts to public services and  that really indicates only one thing  doesn't it  tax rises so the question is when do  they come and where do they fall  i think the priority right now is on  jobs and if  you look at the various steps i've been  taking indeed just a couple of weeks ago  with our winter economy plan  my overwhelming focus at the moment is  trying to protect  and support as many jobs as possible  what's happening in our economy is  significant and severe many people are  losing their jobs so the focus of all my  attention in the short term  is doing what we can to support as much  employment as possible  over time yes you're right we need to  have sustainable public finances  that's important to me it's important to  the government but in the short term  uh the best way to have long-term  sustainable public finance is to try and  protect as much employment as possible  sure but you can understand why people  listening to you yes they might wish to  know where you are focusing on in terms  of the tax rises that seem inevitable  given what you've just said and what you  said yes  and as every interview i do people would  like to know about tax and i give  exactly the same answer every time  i can't comment on future tax policy tax  policy is set at fiscal events  but i think people broadly should be  understanding that  my focus right now is protecting jobs  and employment that's why all the  various  things that we've put in place whether  it's supporting small businesses through  our new pay-as-you-growth scheme to help  restructure bounce-back loans  whether it's the green homes grant  program to help people insulate their  homes and generate activity whether it's  the kickstart scheme to help those  who are young and at risk of long-term  unemployment find job placements  all of that is targeted to the problem  at the moment which is supporting jobs  when you say that we we should be living  without fear right now  what do you mean by that i mean it's  it's fair enough for you know  fit rich good looking 40 year old to say  live without fear  but for those who have lost their jobs  or are about to lose their jobs or  perhaps in one of  the shielding categories it doesn't  resonate it's almost trumpian  we heard this from donald trump in fact  last night  he said don't be afraid of the virus yes  and you also heard from the prime  minister live fearlessly but with  common sense and i fully agree with that  i think it's important that we  maintain some degree of confidence and  optimism about our future  and actually that has an impact on our  economic performance you talk about jobs  actually if we have no confidence and  indeed this is a  consumer-driven economy uh consumption  drives a large part of economic activity  in this country if people don't have  confidence to do those things  that will actually have a direct impact  on people losing their jobs  and it's because of that that i'm so  keen to make sure that we can try and  maintain as much economic activity as  possible that's not an abstract concept  that's about protecting those jobs that  you spoke about and yes you're right  for those people who are losing those  jobs that is tragic it's something  that i feel very deeply and i want to  make sure that we provide all of those  people  with fresh hope and opportunity which is  why our initiatives and new  apprenticeships new training  our kickstart scheme are so important  mentioned a comparison with donald trump  a second ago in terms of the language  that's used another thing that both you  and he seem  very fond of doing is putting your name  on everything admittedly with him it's  on terms of towers  with you it's in terms of the political  advertising we have a look  there have been an awful lot of adverts  over the past few weeks  with your name on with your face on  with the greatest respect you're you're  not the prime minister  so it does almost look just the  teensiest best narcissistic  uh no actually i'm i'm very new to  politics in that sense and actually for  someone who has a job like mine  uh where i'm responsible for the uk  economy i'm not someone who's been in  politics for a very long time and  actually understandably most people  probably didn't know who i was before i  had this job and maybe they don't know  you aren't known  and that's that's exactly the point  right my job is  to make sure that everyone in this  country understands what i'm doing on  their behalf  can question it hold me accountable for  it and feel engaged with that debate  now that that's easier for them to do  that if their politician apart from a  prime minister whoever sticks their name  their personal branding over their  policy announcements  well i think that's that's we're  actually living in a world where people  can communicate in different ways  my main my main desire is to make sure  everyone understands what i'm doing so  they can question it hold me account for  it  know why i'm doing it and i'm going to  try lots of different ways to  communicate with people i'm not going to  apologize for that i want people to know  what we're up to and so that they can  question it and  decide whether they like it don't like  it either way that's the right they have  you mentioned the us actually the vast  majority  of senior politicians in the us take an  approach to social media  which is very similar you just mentioned  one but actually  lots of other politicians and i would go  into a long list of them which most  people may not have even heard  but not over here but the point is the  same but the point is the one about  communication  i mean this is one form of communication  and you'll have your listeners at home  who are watching this  there'll be lots of other people who  might 